{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B013",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B022",
    "B004"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B022 to B013. Two-dimensional conformal field theory remains, in my judgment, a uniquely elegant and universal theoretical achievement. But the discussion makes clearer that B013 combines a sharply stated physical mechanism—the magic-wavelength lattice suppressing motional shifts—with a mature, independently realized experimental capability. Katori and Ye represent complementary conceptual and experimental contributions, and the achievement enables measurements that directly test fundamental physics rather than merely refining a technical standard. This is a sufficiently clean case in which a new physical method, rigorous control, and reproducible consequence remain closely connected.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation is that optical lattice clocks rest on a wider history of optical clocks, frequency metrology, and enabling instrumentation. The award must therefore be confined to the lattice-clock method and its decisive development, without appropriating that broader lineage. There also remains a historical question whether the proposed pair fully captures the original and decisive experimental contributions. B022 retains greater conceptual unity and exactness, while B004 remains a formidable mature theoretical alternative.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that magic-wavelength lattice confinement permits long interrogation of many atoms with controlled motional shifts, and that the resulting clocks are reproducible instruments with fundamental-physics and metrological uses.",
        "response": "I find this account persuasive. It identifies an essential mechanism rather than treating the result as undifferentiated precision engineering. The mechanism, experimental realization, and reproducibility form an unusually coherent chain from physical idea to durable capability, which has led me to place B013 above my original preference for B022."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth stresses that the Katori-Ye attribution should be restricted to optical lattice clocks and not extended to all optical-frequency metrology, while recognizing possible additional foundational contributors.",
        "response": "I agree with both elements. The narrow boundary is indispensable: it preserves the connection between Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's decisive development, while avoiding a claim over the full metrological ecosystem. This restraint is also why the configuration is presently more defensible than a broader award for optical clocks."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson maintains that DMFT is a durable, quantitatively useful framework for correlated electrons, but acknowledges the need to distinguish its foundation from later extensions and broader many-body theory.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B004 as a major achievement and agree that a narrow citation can protect its foundational core. Yet its mean-field approximation and the extensive subsequent development make the precise prize boundary less immediate than for B013. This comparison has strengthened my preference for the experimentally established lattice-clock configuration, not because theory is less deserving, but because the present attribution and achievement boundary are clearer."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review confirms that Hidetoshi Katori and Jun Ye alone constitute the complete and proportionate attribution for the invention and decisive development of optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the final citation can remain sufficiently narrow to distinguish optical lattice clocks from the wider histories of optical clocks, frequency combs, and optical-frequency metrology.",
      "Whether the committee should instead give priority to the more fundamental but primarily theoretical achievement B022."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct conflict is apparent from the supplied profile. I share the Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but no specific collaboration or interest concerning B013 has been identified."
  }
}
